  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: \nWhile the component incorporates several baseline accessibility primitives (native `<button>`, `aria-haspopup`, `aria-expanded`, and Escape key restoration), it falls into the classic \"80% implemented ARIA pattern\" trap. Most critically, DOM focus is placed on the parent `<ul>` with no `aria-activedescendant` or roving focus on options, leaving screen reader users completely unaware of option changes when pressing arrow keys. Combined with the absence of an accessible name on the listbox, missing dismissal on `Tab` or outside clicks, and lack of visual scroll synchronization, the widget fails real assistive technology and keyboard users.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore reviewing the code, custom dropdown / select-only combobox widgets typically exhibit:\n1. *Prediction:* Arrow keys update visual state but fail to announce the active option to screen readers due to missing `aria-activedescendant` or roving tabindex. \u2192 **Confirmed (Critical Finding 1).**\n2. *Prediction:* Missing accessible name on the popup listbox container (`aria-labelledby` / `aria-label`). \u2192 **Confirmed (Major Finding 1).**\n3. *Prediction:* Focus escapes or becomes orphaned when `Tab` is pressed while the popup is open. \u2192 **Confirmed (Major Finding 2).**\n4. *Prediction:* Trigger button accessible name disconnects from external `<label>` or overrides field context with current value. \u2192 **Confirmed (Major Finding 3).**\n5. *Prediction:* Options out of view do not scroll into view during arrow navigation. \u2192 **Confirmed (Major Finding 4).**\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Arrow navigation is completely silent; listbox lacks accessible name; no active descendant linkage. |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | `Tab` leaves popup orphaned; no outside-click/blur dismissal; selected items do not scroll into view; missing `Home`/`End`/typeahead. |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Selected item scrolls out of view in fixed-height container; high-contrast focus ring present on trigger and list but missing per-item indicator. |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Inconsistent dismissal behavior (Escape works, but Tab and outside clicks fail). |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animations or auto-motion present. |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio/video media present. |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | `#0066cc` on white (4.5:1) and `#0066cc` on `#e6f2ff` (4.9:1) meet WCAG AA contrast requirements. |\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. Silent Option Navigation: Missing `aria-activedescendant` and Option IDs\n- **Evidence**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:75-99`\n  ```jsx\n  <ul ref={listRef} id=\"dropdown-listbox\" role=\"listbox\" tabIndex=\"0\" ...>\n    {options.map((option, index) => (\n      <li key={index} role=\"option\" aria-selected={index === selectedIndex} ...>\n  ```\n- **Element**: `<ul role=\"listbox\">` and `<li role=\"option\">`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen Reader Users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value); WAI-ARIA APG Listbox & Select-Only Combobox Pattern\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  The developer noted: *\"Roving tabindex: listbox gets tabindex=\"0\", items not individually focusable\"*. This is a fundamental misunderstanding of ARIA keyboard interaction models. Placing `tabIndex=\"0\"` on the `<ul>` keeps DOM focus frozen on the list container. When the user presses Arrow Down or Arrow Up, React updates `selectedIndex`, which toggles `aria-selected=\"true\"` in the DOM. However, **screen readers do not listen for arbitrary DOM mutations on non-focused children**. Without `aria-activedescendant=\"option-${selectedIndex}\"` on the focused `<ul>` (pointing to each option's unique `id`), or true roving `tabIndex` (`tabIndex={0}` on active item, `-1` on others with `.focus()`), screen readers remain completely silent as the user arrows through options. The user cannot know what option they are highlighting before pressing Enter.\n- **Fix**:\n  Assign unique IDs to each option and bind `aria-activedescendant` to the listbox (or combobox trigger):\n  ```jsx\n  <ul\n    ref={listRef}\n    id=\"dropdown-listbox\"\n    role=\"listbox\"\n    tabIndex=\"0\"\n    aria-activedescendant={`dropdown-option-${selectedIndex}`}\n    aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label\"\n    onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}\n    className=\"dropdown-list\"\n  >\n    {options.map((option, index) => (\n      <li\n        key={index}\n        id={`dropdown-option-${index}`}\n        role=\"option\"\n        aria-selected={index === selectedIndex}\n        onClick={() => handleSelect(index)}\n        className={`dropdown-item ${index === selectedIndex ? 'selected' : ''}`}\n      >\n        {option}\n      </li>\n    ))}\n  </ul>\n  ```\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: dropdown_silent_arrow_navigation\nfingerprint: c8a192b0f44e11\nsource: DropdownSelect.jsx:75-99\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA APG Listbox Pattern / WCAG 4.1.2\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 4.1.2 (Section 508 502.3.1 / 504.2)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=HIGH\nevidence: DropdownSelect.jsx lines 75-99: <ul role=\"listbox\" tabIndex=\"0\"> lacks aria-activedescendant and <li role=\"option\"> elements lack id attributes.\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Open dropdown using Space/Enter/ArrowDown.\n  2. Press ArrowDown to navigate through options while running NVDA or VoiceOver.\n  3. Observe that screen reader does not announce the newly selected option.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader must announce each option's label and state as the user arrows through the listbox.\nactual_behavior: DOM focus remains stationary on <ul>; no active descendant notification is dispatched; screen reader is silent.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Unnamed Listbox Container\n- **Evidence**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:75-82`\n- **Element**: `<ul id=\"dropdown-listbox\" role=\"listbox\" ...>`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen Reader Users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value); SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  When focus moves to `listRef.current?.focus()`, the screen reader announces the newly focused widget. Because the `<ul>` lacks `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label`, assistive technologies announce an unnamed listbox (or VoiceOver may read the entire concatenated string of all options).\n- **Fix**:\n  Add an `id=\"dropdown-label\"` to the `<label>` and associate it to the listbox via `aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label\"`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: listbox_missing_accessible_name\nfingerprint: a4b99827df103c\nsource: DropdownSelect.jsx:75-82\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 4.1.2\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH\nevidence: DropdownSelect.jsx line 78: <ul id=\"dropdown-listbox\" role=\"listbox\"> has no aria-labelledby or aria-label.\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Inspect accessibility tree or focus the <ul> element in VoiceOver/NVDA.\n  2. Check the computed accessible name of role=\"listbox\".\nexpected_behavior: The listbox should compute its accessible name from the component's label (e.g., \"Choose an option\").\nactual_behavior: Computed accessible name is empty.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 2. Tab Key and Outside-Click Leave Popup Orphaned (Missing Dismissal & Focus Escape)\n- **Evidence**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:38-56`\n- **Element**: `handleKeyDown` and missing `useEffect` outside-click listener\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only Users, Low Vision Users, Screen Reader Users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.3 (Focus Order), SC 2.4.11 (Focus Not Obscured), WAI-ARIA APG Listbox Keyboard Interaction\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  If a user presses `Tab` while in the listbox, `handleKeyDown` does not intercept `Tab`. Focus moves to the next focusable control in the DOM, but `isOpen` remains `true`! The listbox remains visually pinned over underlying content, obscuring subsequent form elements. Furthermore, clicking outside does not close the dropdown. Per APG, pressing `Tab` while the popup is open must close the listbox (either retaining selection or committing the active option) and allow focus to advance naturally.\n- **Fix**:\n  Handle `Tab` within `handleKeyDown` to close the listbox before focus advances:\n  ```jsx\n  } else if (e.key === 'Tab') {\n    setIsOpen(false);\n    // Allow default tab navigation to next element\n  }\n  ```\n  And add an outside click listener (`pointerdown`) in a `useEffect` hook to close the dropdown when interacting elsewhere.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: dropdown_orphaned_on_tab_out\nfingerprint: f128d9c0234ea5\nsource: DropdownSelect.jsx:38-56\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.3 Focus Order, SC 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 2.4.3\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM\nevidence: DropdownSelect.jsx lines 38-56: handleKeyDown does not handle 'Tab'; no blur or document click listener exists to close listbox.\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Open dropdown via Enter/Space.\n  2. Press Tab.\n  3. Observe that focus moves to the next page element, but dropdown listbox remains open and visible.\nexpected_behavior: Tabbing away from the open listbox must dismiss the popup.\nactual_behavior: Listbox remains open, overlaying and obscuring subsequent page content.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 3. Trigger Accessible Name Disconnected from Label\n- **Evidence**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:60-73`\n  ```jsx\n  <label htmlFor=\"dropdown-button\">{label}</label>\n  <button id=\"dropdown-button\" ...>\n    {options[selectedIndex]}\n    <span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u25bc</span>\n  </button>\n  ```\n- **Element**: `<label>` and `<button id=\"dropdown-button\">`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen Reader Users, Cognitive Accessibility Users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value); HTML-AAM \u00a74.1\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  In HTML-AAM, `<button>` elements calculate their accessible name from their subtree (its text contents: `{options[selectedIndex]}`). While `<label for=\"button-id\">` is valid in HTML5, many assistive technologies (e.g., VoiceOver on Safari) do NOT announce the `<label>` text as the primary accessible name of a button\u2014they announce only the button text (`\"Option 1, collapsed, button\"`). Screen reader users navigating by form controls or buttons miss the field label entirely (they hear \"California\" instead of \"State: California\").\n- **Fix**:\n  Explicitly connect the label and current value using `aria-labelledby`:\n  ```jsx\n  <label id=\"dropdown-label\" htmlFor=\"dropdown-button\">{label}</label>\n  <button\n    ref={buttonRef}\n    id=\"dropdown-button\"\n    aria-haspopup=\"listbox\"\n    aria-expanded={isOpen}\n    aria-controls=\"dropdown-listbox\"\n    aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label dropdown-button\"\n    ...\n  >\n  ```\n\n#### 4. Visual Selection Not Scrolled into View\n- **Evidence**: CSS lines `45-59` (`.dropdown-list { max-height: 200px; overflow-y: auto; }`) and `DropdownSelect.jsx:39-46`\n- **Element**: `.dropdown-list` scroll container and arrow key navigation\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only Users, Low Vision Users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.7 (Focus Visible), SC 2.4.11 (Focus Not Obscured)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  The listbox container is constrained to `max-height: 200px` with `overflow-y: auto`. When the options list has 10+ items, pressing Arrow Down increments `selectedIndex`, but does not call `scrollIntoView()` on the active item. Once navigation moves past the 4th item, the highlighted item disappears below the viewport. The keyboard user can no longer see what is selected.\n- **Fix**:\n  Add an effect or ref-based call to ensure the active option scrolls into view when `selectedIndex` changes:\n  ```jsx\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (isOpen && listRef.current) {\n      const activeItem = listRef.current.querySelector(`[aria-selected=\"true\"]`);\n      activeItem?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });\n    }\n  }, [selectedIndex, isOpen]);\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction with Workarounds)\n\n1. **Dead Code on List Items**:\n   - `DropdownSelect.jsx:89-94`: Each `<li>` defines an `onKeyDown` handler for `Enter`. Because the `<li>` elements have no `tabIndex` and are not focused directly, this event handler is dead code.\n2. **Missing `Home` and `End` Keyboard Navigation**:\n   - Per WAI-ARIA APG Listbox keyboard conventions, `Home` should jump to the first option and `End` should jump to the last option.\n3. **Missing Typeahead Support**:\n   - In standard select widgets and APG listboxes, typing alphanumeric characters jumps directly to the matching option. Without typeahead, keyboard users with long lists face significant navigation friction.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice Polish)\n\n- **WAI-ARIA Pattern Alignment (Combobox vs. Listbox)**:\n  The current component is an ambiguous hybrid: it opens a popup listbox, but transfers DOM focus directly into the `<ul>`. In modern WAI-ARIA APG (Select-Only Combobox), the trigger button itself retains `role=\"combobox\"` with `aria-autocomplete=\"none\"`, and focus remains on the trigger while `aria-activedescendant` coordinates with the popup. Keeping focus on the trigger avoids unmounting/focus-restoration race conditions.\n- **`preventScroll` on Trigger Restoration**:\n  When restoring focus to the button (`buttonRef.current?.focus()`), pass `{ preventScroll: true }` to avoid unexpected page jumping on longer forms.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gaps & Unstated Assumptions)\n\n- **Missing Outside-Click Dismissal**: No event listener on `document` to close the dropdown when a user clicks outside the component boundaries.\n- **Missing `Tab` Dismissal**: No closing mechanism when keyboard focus tabs out of the open listbox.\n- **Missing DOM ID Association**: `<li role=\"option\">` elements lack IDs, preventing assistive technologies from tracking active selection.\n- **Missing Accessible Name for Listbox**: `<ul>` has `role=\"listbox\"` with no `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`.\n- **Missing Scroll-into-View Behavior**: No programmatic scroll adjustment for long lists in the scrollable container.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen Reader User (NVDA / VoiceOver / JAWS)**:\n  Opening the dropdown announces an unnamed listbox. As the user presses the Down Arrow, the screen reader remains completely silent because DOM focus is static on the `<ul>` and no `aria-activedescendant` is emitted. The user has no indication of what is being selected.\n- **Keyboard-only User**:\n  Opening and navigating via arrows works in small lists, but if the user presses `Tab` to exit, the dropdown remains permanently open on screen, covering subsequent inputs. On long lists, the selection vanishes below the scroll container fold.\n- **Low Vision User**:\n  The focus ring contrast (3px solid `#0066cc`) is excellent on the trigger and listbox. However, when zoomed in to 200%, the lack of `scrollIntoView` means options quickly become obscured outside the 200px scroll window.\n- **Cognitive Accessibility User**:\n  Inconsistent dismissal semantics (Escape closes it, but clicking outside or pressing Tab does not) cause confusion regarding whether a selection was made or aborted.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\nThe component is rated **REVISE**. \nAlthough the trigger button implementation includes valid attributes (`aria-expanded`, `aria-haspopup`, `aria-controls`), the interaction model breaks down inside the listbox:\n1. Navigating options via arrow keys is silent to screen readers (Critical).\n2. The listbox lacks an accessible name (Major).\n3. Tab navigation and outside clicks leave an orphaned popup blocking the page (Major).\n4. Long lists hide active options without scrolling (Major).\n\nTo upgrade the verdict to **ACCEPT**, the component must implement `aria-activedescendant` on the listbox pointing to unique option IDs, associate `aria-labelledby` with the listbox, dismiss the popup on `Tab` / outside clicks, and scroll active options into view.\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n1. *Expected Selection Model*: Should pressing `Tab` while open commit the currently highlighted option (standard OS select behavior) or cancel and restore the previous selection?\n2. *Form Submission*: Is this component intended to integrate into a standard HTML `<form>`? If so, a hidden `<input name=\"...\" value=\"...\">` is needed so values submit without JavaScript formData extraction.\n",
